"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT3b, demonstrating extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and lymph node metastasis. The preoperative PSA was 8.3. The biopsy findings included Gleason 4+3 and Gleason 3+5 patterns. The surgical findings indicate a high-risk profile, with the surgical report noting \"pathological stage pT3b. Extraprostatic extension was present; surgical margins were positive; the seminal vesicles were invaded; lymph node metastasis was present.\"\n\nSeveral factors drive the prognosis, including the positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and documented lymph node metastasis. Conversely, the documented nodal status is positive, which is a significant negative prognostic factor. The preoperative PSA of 8.3 is also a factor. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy differences are noted, with the biopsy showing higher Gleason scores than the final pathology. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up information would revise the prognosis, particularly regarding the subsequent trajectory of the PSA. The uncertainty in the prediction is reflected in the heuristic sensitivity range for recurrence time. The fixed estimated horizon is 31.78 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 25.42–38.13 months; its coverage has not been validated."
